  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The offense produced two runs on base and still left, trailing after the sixth for good. Mahle gave them five clean innings, the bullpen answered with six straight scoreless, and they still lost to Kansas City because ten strikeouts and five hits don't amount to anything when you need runs. This is the inconsistency that's been running through the season. Not collapse, but incompleteness. They had chances in the first and seventh with runners in scoring position. They converted one. The other inning folded. That gap, that half-measure, that's what four straight losses look like from inside the clubhouse.",
    "what_this_means": "The problem isn't the pitching. Mahle moved through five innings cleanly while the bullpen locked down Kansas City for 2.3 more, giving the lineup multiple windows to work. The offense punched back in the fifth, taking a one-run lead. That's when the game was theirs to lose. And they did, in the sixth, when two more runs crossed and the lead evaporated just as fast as it arrived. You don't get a second chance in a one-run game when your bats go quiet after one good inning. The bullpen was ready. The lineup wasn't there to answer. Ten strikeouts against five hits says everything about where the real damage lived.",
    "what_to_watch": "Landen Roupp takes the ball tonight against Seth Lugo and Kansas City again. Roupp's last four starts carry a 3.49 ERA. Control under pressure, exactly what a team this close to the division can't afford to waste.",
